n a Jenkins Declarative Pipeline, the post block is a section that runs automatically after all the main stages of your pipeline have finished executing.

Think of it as a safety net or a final clean-up crew. No matter what happened during the build—whether your tests failed, your Docker push succeeded, or the pipeline crashed halfway through—the code inside the post block is guaranteed to run.

Inside your snippet, you are using the always condition

post {
    always {
        // Runs no matter what (Success, Failure, or Aborted)
    }
}
Other common conditions you can use in a post block include success, failure, unstable, or changed

What is cleanWs()?
cleanWs() stands for "Clean Workspace." It is a command provided by the Workspace Cleanup Plugin in Jenkins.

When Jenkins runs a pipeline on an agent (like your EC2 instance), it creates a dedicated folder called a workspace for that specific job. Into this workspace, it clones your Git repository, downloads dependencies, compiles Java files via Maven, and builds local Docker artifacts.

If you don't clean up this workspace after every build, several bad things happen:

Disk Space Exhaustion: Over time, heavy files (like target/ folders, uncompressed node modules, or local Docker cache files) will pile up on your EC2 instance's hard drive until the server runs out of disk space and crashes.

State Pollution / Stale Files: Leftover temporary files from a previous failed build might accidentally interfere with or get packaged into your next fresh build, causing strange, unpredictable bugs.

post {
    always {
        echo 'Cleaning up workspace container artifacts...'
        cleanWs()    
    }
}
